{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Maroon Wars''' ({{Lang-bl|Guerras marrón}}) also known the '''''Intifada''''' ({{Literal translation|the uprising}}) is a term used in Baliscan historiography to refer to successful {{W|insurrection}}s by {{W|slave revolt|self-liberated slaves}} against [[Colonial Balisca|Mesogean colonial rule]] in the [[Northern Triangle]]. The first revolts began immediately following the introduction of slavery in [[Bayara]] (1526) and ended in 1809 with the destruction of these institutions and the relatively violent expulsion of former facilitators of chattel slavery during the opening years of the [[Baliscan War of Independence|Volisanian attempt at reconquest]]. 

The Maroon Wars were distinguished from other slave revolts in the colonial world by the role of the [[Catharism|Catharist]] communities of [[Cerdanya]], whose early abolition of slavery in 1611 created a territorial sanctuary for self-liberating people and whose missionary network provided the literacy, material support, and theological framework that enabled the Maroon resistance to expand from isolated revolts into a coordinated revolutionary movement. The convergence of Cathar literacy campaigns with the existing literacy of enslaved [[Islam in Balisca|Muslim]] Kaftians—estimated at sixty percent of the enslaved population—produced a uniquely capable resistance network that could communicate, plan, and administer across the archipelago's difficult terrain. 

The early successes of the revolts had a long-lasting impact on the history of the Araucarian territory and the history of Abyala, determining the shape of future colonial relations with self-liberated Kaftians and Indigenous peoples, e.g. by causing the consolidation of the Araucarian [[W:Frontier|frontier]]. The effects of these conflicts on the institution of slavery were felt throughout the [[New World]], with the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] and the expansion of [[Maroon piracy]]. The rebels' organizational capacity and tenacity under pressure ultimately contributed to the 1809 [[Granyana Proclamation]] which ''{{W|de jure}}'' abolished the practice of slavery across the Northern Triangle.

== Background ==

=== Slavery in the Northern Triangle ===
{{Main|Aethiopian slave trade|Slavery in Balisca}}
The trans-Kaftian slave trade reached the Northern Triangle in the early 16th century, as colonial authorities—relying heavily on Mesogean slave traffickers—imported enslaved Kaftians to work the sugar, cotton, and tobacco plantations of [[Bayara]]. In 1521, Volisanian colonial authorities had passed ordinances attempting to limit the number of Muslim Kaftians taken to their colonies, recognizing that many of these individuals were [[W:Captives of war|captives of war]] (from [[Owuro-Imfezzan wars|wars between the Owuro and Imfezzans]] of [[Central Kaftia]]) with military experience and were viewed as particularly dangerous. The colonies' desire for slave labor outweighed their fears, which were well-founded as enslaved Kaftians—Muslims and non-Muslims alike—continued to rebel.

An estimated sixty percent of enslaved people brought to Balisca from West and Central Kaftia were Muslims. Many were literate in [[Bayani language|Bayani]] and could read and write in the [[W:Arabic script|Bayani script]], a fact that distinguished them from many of their enslavers and that would prove decisive in the development of organized resistance. The literacy of enslaved Muslims enabled communication across plantations, the forging of passes and freedom documents, and the preservation of military and strategic knowledge in written form—capabilities that colonial authorities repeatedly attempted and failed to suppress.

=== The Cathar settlement ===
{{main|Catharism|Catharist migration to Abyala}}

The [[Catharism|Cathar]] presence in the New World traces its roots to the late 15th and early 16th centuries, when [[Mozbayani language|Mozbayani]]-speaking Cathar refugees—facing suspicion, violence, and oppression in Eastern [[Illypnia]] during the [[Andalusi–Mauretanian wars]]—were granted refuge in the [[Volisanian Empire|Kingdom of Volisania]] and subsequently permitted to settle in the Abyalan colonies following the [[Great Disappointment]]. The Cathars' experience of persecution in Illypnia had produced a deeply held opposition to institutional violence and a theological commitment to ''[[W:Consolamentum|consolamentum]]''—the consolatory ritual that was the central sacrament of Cathar faith—which emphasized the inherent dignity of the human spirit against material corruption.

The Cathars settled primarily in [[Estuaria]] and [[Cerdanya]], occupying a geographic niche that was viewed as undesirable by other Illypnian colonists, creating conditions more amenable to their communal and ascetic way of life. Compared to other areas of Balisca, very few enslaved Kaftians and Muslims were brought to Cerdanya following the beginning of the Kaftian slave trade (mostly enslaved people attached to arriving trading vessels), a fact that reflected both the region's economic character (oriented toward fishing, trade, and small-scale agriculture rather than plantation monoculture) and the Cathars' influence on colonial governance.

Cathar theology held slavery to be a [[W:Mortal sin|mortal sin]]—a manifestation of the material world's corruption of the spirit. While the Cathars were generally committed to nonviolence, their theological tradition distinguished between the initiation of violence (which was sinful) and the resistance to oppression (which was a spiritual obligation). This distinction, elaborated in the [[Treatise on the Just Resistance]] ({{lang|oc|Tractat de la Justa Resisténcia}}) composed by the Cathar perfectus [[Bernat de Calvià]] around 1580, provided the theological framework that would later justify and motivate Cathar support for the Maroon resistance.

==== Cerdanya's abolition of slavery ====
The growing tension between Cathar anti-slavery conviction and the colonial slave economy culminated in 1611, when the [[Captaincy General of Cerdanya]]—under the influence of its Cathar merchant and religious elite—outlawed [[W:Chattel slavery|chattel slavery]] within its jurisdiction. The 1611 abolition decree, the first of its kind in Abyala and the earliest in the colonial world, declared that "no human soul may be held as property, for the spirit is the domain of God alone and the body its temporary vessel."

The decree's immediate effect was to transform Cerdanya into a [[W:Slave states and free states|territorial sanctuary]] for self-liberating people. Following the outbreak of the Maroon conflict, a sizable number of runaway slaves were able to flee to Cerdanya, where they were immediately declared free. The Cerdanyan authorities—dominated by the Cathar community—refused all requests from Bayaran authorities and other slave-holding entities for the return of fugitives, establishing a principle of territorial freedom that would define Cerdanya's role throughout the Maroon Wars.

The abolition provoked a crisis in colonial relations. Bayara's planter class demanded the revocation of the decree and the return of fugitive property; the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisanian Crown]], reluctant to incite inter-colonial fighting that could undermine its tenuous hold on Abylan colonies in the face of competition with Fosian powers, prevaricated. The result was an uneasy compromise: Cerdanya's abolition was permitted to stand, but the Crown prohibited its extensioin to other captaincies, effectively creating a free territory within a slave colony and guaranteeing that the Maroon resistance would have a secure base of operations.

